---
title: Browser library
description: The GateKeeper vanilla JavaScript package: browser-safe session refresh, profile calls, MFA APIs, and custom elements.
section: Application libraries
---

`@gatekeeper/browser` is the vanilla JavaScript integration package for GateKeeper
applications. It is written in TypeScript, builds to plain ESM JavaScript, and has no Svelte,
React, Vue, or server-runtime dependency.

The package source lives at `packages/gatekeeper-browser`.

## Browser-only boundary

This package runs in the user's browser, so it deliberately does **not** implement the server-side
parts from the SvelteKit BFF package:

- No encrypted session cookie implementation.
- No client secret handling.
- No refresh-token storage.
- No server-to-server GateKeeper calls.
- No framework request hooks or server loaders.

Instead, it calls same-origin backend-for-frontend endpoints that your application owns. Those
endpoints should keep tokens in `httpOnly` server sessions and return only browser-safe state.

## Create the browser client

```ts
import { createGateKeeperBrowserClient } from '@gatekeeper/browser';

const gatekeeper = createGateKeeperBrowserClient({
	initialSession: window.__GATEKEEPER_SESSION__,
	sessionEndpoint: '/api/gatekeeper/session',
	profileEndpoint: '/api/me/profile',
	refreshSkewSeconds: 120
});
```

The client exposes:

- `getSnapshot()`, `getSession()`, `getUser()`, and `isAuthenticated()`.
- `refresh()` for an immediate session refresh.
- `startAutoRefresh()` and `stopAutoRefresh()`.
- `subscribe()` and `on()` for UI state updates.
- `updateUserProfile()` for the signed-in user's profile.

By default, auto-refresh is enabled. The client schedules a `GET` request to
`/api/gatekeeper/session` before `expiresAt`, using the configured `refreshSkewSeconds`.

## Session endpoint contract

The refresh endpoint should return this browser-safe shape:

```ts
type BrowserSafeSession = {
	authenticated: boolean;
	user: GateKeeperProfile | null;
	expiresAt: number | null;
};
```

Do not include refresh tokens. Access tokens should also stay server-side unless your application
has explicitly chosen a browser-token architecture. The client has `getAccessToken()` only for
applications that intentionally include a short-lived `accessToken` field in the response.

With the SvelteKit BFF package, the endpoint looks like this:

```ts
import { json } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import { gatekeeper } from '$lib/server/gatekeeper';

export const GET = async ({ locals, cookies }) => {
	const event = { locals, cookies };
	await gatekeeper.loadSession(event);
	return json(gatekeeper.getBrowserSession(event));
};
```

## Update profile data

```ts
await gatekeeper.updateUserProfile({
	displayName: 'Ada Lovelace',
	firstName: 'Ada',
	lastName: 'Lovelace'
});
```

The default endpoint is `PUT /api/me/profile`. It should authenticate with the server-side session,
call GateKeeper, and return the updated profile.

## MFA headless API

```ts
import { createMfaClient } from '@gatekeeper/browser';

const mfa = createMfaClient({ basePath: '/api/mfa' });

const state = await mfa.listMethods();
await mfa.setPreferredMethod('webauthn');
const totp = await mfa.startTotpEnrollment();
await mfa.confirmTotpEnrollment('123456');
await mfa.addPasskey('YubiKey 5C');
```

The API matches the Svelte helper shape, so developers can move between framework-native and
framework-agnostic UI without changing backend endpoints.

## Use custom elements

For no-framework pages, register the elements:

```ts
import { defineGateKeeperElements } from '@gatekeeper/browser/elements';

defineGateKeeperElements();
```

Wrap the app with the provider:

```html
<gatekeeper-provider
	session-endpoint="/api/gatekeeper/session"
	profile-endpoint="/api/me/profile"
	refresh-skew-seconds="120"
>
	<main id="app"></main>
</gatekeeper-provider>
```

The provider dispatches `gatekeeper:change` with the current snapshot whenever session state
changes.

Add the default MFA component:

```html
<gatekeeper-mfa-configuration base-path="/api/mfa"></gatekeeper-mfa-configuration>
```

The MFA component renders light DOM with stable `gk-mfa__*` classes, so the consuming app can style
it directly:

```css
.gk-mfa__section {
	border: 1px solid var(--border);
	padding: 1rem;
}

.gk-mfa__button--primary {
	background: var(--brand);
	color: white;
}
```

Consumers who need fully custom UI should use `createMfaClient()` instead of the custom element.
